/**
 * C1: ESTONIA BEYOND TALLINN (docs/43-lessons.md §15.3, part 7).
 *
 * Summer, the family's third. Kati takes Tom and Anna to her grandparents'
 * farm in Võrumaa, where the old people speak Võro; a guide walks them across
 * a bog and tells its legend; a Seto choir sings leelo at a farm museum; the
 * village inn holds a quiz night on the day the morning show had a folklorist
 * on; a ferry to Saaremaa, and a piece for Kati's blog; and the fair in
 * Setomaa, where Kalju tells Tom how things were.
 *
 * One pattern is explained here and nowhere else in the level: reading the old
 * and the regional (`literary`, lesson 39), the i-plural, the stem plural, the
 * short illative of a song and a dialect word, all read and never produced. The
 * part deepens B2's quotative and pluperfect into the way a legend is told
 * (lesson 38), and its conversation move is working a word out from context
 * and checking it without stopping the story (§15.5), taught in lesson 37 and
 * met again at the fair. Dialect is only ever read, in a text or a talk, with
 * its standard word beside it (`DIALECT_WORDS`); no voice puts on an accent,
 * and Kalju speaks standard Estonian, slowly, with old words.
 *
 * The part's ten-minute talk, the paper's lecture taken down as notes, is
 * lesson 40's: a folklorist on the morning show, heard once.
 *
 * Who is who is `docs/reviews/c1-cast.md`. Six strangers here are new names or
 * role names used in one lesson only: the guide on the bog (`Giid`, lesson
 * 38), the Seto lead singer (`Eeslaulja`, lesson 39), the folklorist on the
 * morning show (`Ülle`, lesson 40), the quizmaster (`Viktoriinijuht`, lesson
 * 40), the man on the ferry (`Saarlane`, lesson 41) and Kalju's neighbor at the
 * fair (`Naaber`, lesson 42).
 *
 * In preview (`preview.ts`) until C1 is the course, and held to every check a
 * lesson is (`npx tsx scripts/check-lesson-part.ts --part c1-maa`).
 */

